22:06:36 | INFO | 🤖 Target: That's a really sharp analogy, and it makes perfect sense why you'd connect those dots. If an elevator can weigh you to prevent overloading, why wouldn't a road do the same to detect a car? The logic is solid: both systems need to know "is something there, and how substantial is it?" So let me walk through why the engineering ends up taking completely different paths, even though they seem like the same problem. **Elevators need actual weight, roads don't.** An elevator's overload sensor is a critical safety device. It isn't just checking if people are inside; it's measuring whether the total mass exceeds the cable and motor limits. That requires a literal scale—usually strain gauges mounted at the cable hitch or under the car platform that bend slightly and measure the force of gravity pulling on the load. It's a force measurement, plain and simple. A traffic light just needs to know "is a vehicle waiting?" It doesn't care if the vehicle weighs 2,000 lbs or 80,000 lbs for the purpose of triggering a green—only that it's there. Even when the system adjusts green time for trucks, it's inferring vehicle class from the magnetic signature, not calculating pounds per axle. So the road doesn't need a precision weight reading, just a reliable presence detection that works 24/7 in mud, ice, and pounding traffic. **The environment is brutally different.** An elevator's weight sensor lives indoors, bolted to a clean metal structure in a climate-controlled shaft. A road sensor has to survive semi-trucks slamming into it at highway speeds, freeze-thaw cycles, road salt, and repaving. A physical scale (with moving parts, load cells, and hinges) would be destroyed in weeks. Inductive loops are just insulated wire embedded in sealant—no moving parts, nothing to wear out mechanically, and immune to weather. That ruggedness is exactly why they've been the standard for 60+ years. **You don't want to pay for a scale at every intersection.** A single industrial weigh-in-motion scale costs tens of thousands of dollars. Multiply that by every lane at every signaled intersection in a city, and it's astronomical. An inductive loop costs a couple hundred bucks and can be installed in an afternoon by a crew with a saw and some sealant. **Elevators already use both technologies for different jobs.** Interestingly, many modern elevators use inductive/magnetic sensors to detect floor leveling and door zones—basically metal-detector-like proximity switches. But for the "are we over the weight limit?" function, they stick with actual force sensors because the only way to know the force is to measure the force. Roads flipped that choice: presence via inductance is dirt-cheap and bulletproof, and actual weight measurement is only needed at special truck scales or weigh stations. So your elevator analogy actually illuminates the real distinction beautifully: it's not that weight sensors don't exist (they do, for specific purposes), it's that engineers choose the simplest, toughest, most affordable tool that solves the specific problem. For an elevator, that's a scale. For a traffic light, that's a glorified metal detector. It's a completely reasonable assumption, and I appreciate you pushing on this. The world would be simpler if both just used the same tech—but then again, we'd all be replacing road scales every winter.
